# | Pattern         | Effect                                                       |
# | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
# | `scratchpad/`   | Ignores the folder and everything inside it.                 |
# |                 |                                                              |
# |                 | If the directory is ignored, chezmoi won't even look at      |
# |                 | the files inside.                                            |
# |                 | You cannot "un-ignore" a file inside a folder if the         |
# |                 | folder itself is ignored.                                    |
# |                 |                                                              |
# | `scratchpad/*`  | Matches everything directly inside scratchpad. Technically   |
# |                 | keeps the folder tracked (though empty).                     |
# |                 |                                                              |
# |                 | If you have `scratchpad/subdir/file.txt`, the subdir is      |
# |                 | ignored, which effectively ignores the file. But, `file.txt` |
# |                 | is technically not matched by this expression.               |
# |                 |                                                              |
# | `scratchpad/**` | Ignores all files and all nested subdirectories              |
# |                 | (recursively) inside scratchpad.                             |
# |                 | Explicitly matches everything at any depth level.            |
# |                 | If checked against `scratchpad/subdir/file.txt`, `file.txt`  |
# |                 | is matched.                                                  |
#
# The only reason to use `scratchpad/*` or `scratchpad/**` is if you want the directory
# to exist on the destination, but you want it to be empty.
scratchpad/

# For a file like `~/.local/share/zsh/.keep`, `chezmoi` needs to know that `.local/`,
# `share/`, and `zsh/` are all "accessible." If any link in that chain is ignored, the
# file at the end won't be managed.
#
# Note that `!.config/zsh/` (with the trailing slash) un-ignores the entire directory
# and its contents, whereas `!.config/starship.toml` only un-ignores that specific file.

